Node.js (recommended for your frontend / edge service):
```js
// install: npm install @redwood/inference
const Redwood = require('@redwood/inference');
const client = new Redwood({ apiKey: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY, region: 'us-west' });

async function sendForAssist(prompt) {
  // single-request pattern with timeout + retry wrapper
  const opts = { timeoutMs: 8000 }; // 8s socket timeout for initial POC
  return retryWithBackoff(() => client.chat.generate({ model: 'rw-medium-2026', input: prompt }), 3, opts);
}

async function retryWithBackoff(fn, attempts = 3, opts = {}) {
  let backoff = 200;
  for (let i = 0; i < attempts; i++) {
    try {
      const res = await Promise.race([fn(), timeout(opts.timeoutMs)]);
      return res;
    } catch (err) {
      if (i === attempts - 1) throw err;
      // on 429 or transient 5xx, wait and retry
      await sleep(backoff);
      backoff *= 2;
    }
  }
}

// helpers (implementations omitted for brevity)
function timeout(ms) { return new Promise((_, rej) => setTimeout(() => rej(new Error('timeout')), ms)); }
function sleep(ms) { return new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, ms)); }
```

Python (backend batch job / embedding upload):
```py
# install: pip install redwood-inference
from redwood import Client
import os
client = Client(api_key=os.getenv('REDWOOD_API_KEY'), region='us-west')

# embeddings: use batched uploads of 512 vectors per request for cost/throughput balance
def upload_vectors(batch):
    resp = client.embeddings.create(model='rw-embed-2026', input=batch, timeout=30)
    return resp

# recommended: use a worker queue (celery/rq) and exponential backoff on 429s
```

Endpoint migration checklist (sandbox -> prod):
- Verify API key scopes and rotation policy.
- Confirm IP allowlist entries and test network connectivity to prod endpoints.
- Switch to prod base-url: https://api.redwood.ai/v1 and set model pins (do not use latest tag in prod).
- Run canary traffic with 5% of requests for 1 hour, compare p99 latency and error rates.
- Validate logging and cost-metering (token counts) for sample transactions.

Retries / timeouts guidance (short):
- Client-side timeouts: socket-level 6-10s for interactive flows; 30s+ for bulk embedding operations.
- Retries: up to 3 attempts for 429/5xx with exponential backoff starting at 200ms. Add jitter.
- Respect Retry-After header when present. For rate-limit spikes use client-side adaptive windowing (reduce concurrency for 10s).

Rate limit handling (Dedicated & Hosted):
- Hosted: soft limits per API key; we recommend token buckets at the SDK layer and a leaky queue for spikes.
- Dedicated: you can set guaranteed throughput; we will share recommended per-instance RPS for your traffic shape if you provide a short load profile.

Let me know if you want more robust streaming examples or a traffic-shaping helper function. I can bring a short demo to the call.

